      lead: "Some rides end at the finish. Others stay with you long afterwards.",
      note: "For the fourth year running, we celebrated Midsummer with our traditional long ride.\n\nThis time, the route led from Moscow to Ivanovo via Kirzhach, Yuryev-Polsky and Gavrilov Posad.\n\nThe plan was ambitious but clear: 300+ km at an average of 35 km/h. In the end, it went even better. We covered the main part of the route at an average of 36.5 km/h, and the total distance was 310 km.\n\nOnce, the very idea of riding 300, 400, 500 or even 1001 km in a single day seemed mad. Now it feels like a big journey you don’t want to end.\n\nAnd it has nothing to do with fitness. With the right people beside you, a well-judged pace and good organisation, a long distance stops being frightening and starts being enjoyable.\n\nThe highlight came at around kilometre 270, in Teykovo.\n\nWe’re riding through town. Suddenly, a traffic police car blasts past us with its siren on. My first thought was a very down-to-earth one: “Could these be the same guys who recently fined our support van?”\n\nBut what happened next was something nobody expected.\n\nThe crew drives ahead, blocks the intersection so the whole group can ride through safely, then escorts us for several kilometres all the way out of town, clearing the traffic.\n\nTrips like these make you especially\nappreciate two things.\n\nThe first is the pace.\nThere were just 12 of us. Everyone understood that smoothness had long since become the new speed. No surges, no fuss, no pointless battles with each other. Stops were planned too: every 50–60 km in the first half of the route and roughly every 40 km closer to the finish.\n\nThe second is the road.\nAfter Chernogolovka, the route becomes simply magnificent. Start early in the morning, as we did at 5:30, and you enjoy literally every kilometre.\n\nI definitely recommend the route.\n<a href=\"https://www.komoot.com/tour/3056140739/edit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">📍Route on Komoot</a>:\n\nDays like these don’t happen by themselves.\nThank you to <a href=\"https://t.me/sunr1ser\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Viktor</a> for bringing everyone together again and leading this masterpiece of a ride.\n<a href=\"https://t.me/Romchillin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">@Romchillin</a> and <a href=\"https://t.me/mio_bikelover\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">@mio_bikelover</a> for the organisation and attention to every detail.\n<a href=\"https://t.me/Rebellion_Sports\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Rebellion</a> - for the coffee and that cake, which tasted especially good after a couple of hundred kilometres.\n<a href=\"https://t.me/karachenkov\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">@karachenkov</a> - for the support.\n\nWhat is the most unexpected thing a stranger has done for you on the road?\n\nP.S.\n\nThe Midsummer 300+ story was far from over for everyone.\nThe others stayed in Ivanovo to rest and recover. Kirill and Stepan took the Lastochka back to Moscow to take care of their own things.\n\nMy adventure, though, was only just beginning.\nOn the morning of June 28 I ran DDLONG, and that same night Konstantin and I flew to the Altai Mountains.\n\nAhead lay the Chuysky Trakt, almost 900 kilometres, more than 9000 metres of elevation gain and five unforgettable days.\nSometimes the best rest after a big ride is not to stop. Just to change the route.\n\nBut that’s another story…",
